% ======================================================================================== 1 Summary
\section{Introduction and summary}\label{sec:summary}
A camera films the court; a model finds the ball, fits its flight and calls the point before the bounce
(Fig.~\ref{fig:teaser}). A Polymarket price \emph{is} the chance that a player wins and lags each point by about a
second, so whoever learns the point first sets the price. We map who is fast, price each second of feed delay
(<< V('cv.pre.persec.range') >> a day for the first) and test it on a pre-registered fresh holdout.

\para{Our Sharpe ratios are above 3, so we hunted for bugs} v2's << V('v2.is.sr') >> comes from about
<< V('v2.trades_per_day') >> small bets a day on points already played, held to resolution, with a mean same-day
correlation of << V('risk.corr_sameday') >>, so daily P\&L is rarely negative; the CV trader is the same kind of event
book. We found one bug: the score-move detector's start time used later prices (D9); the fix cut the in-sample Sharpe
from << V('v2.onset.is.sr') >> to << V('v2.is.sr') >>. Then: at zero feed delay the latency sweep reproduces the
published CV result exactly (the \(V = 0\) identity); copying the same trades 3\,s later loses (<< V('copy3.c.is') >>¢
a share IS), as does a copier filled after the << V('copier.lag') >>\,s block lag and the venue's hold
(<< V('copier.is.c') >>¢ IS, << V('copier.oos.c') >>¢ OOS); our causal engine, with no look-ahead feature, nearly matches
Table~\ref{tab:head}'s 1\,s cells; and << V('snow.repro') >> paper numbers re-derive in SQL (<< V('snow.recomputed') >> recomputed from rows,
<< V('snow.lookup') >> looked up).

