% ======================================================================================== 2 Hypothesis
\section{Economic hypothesis}\label{sec:hyp}
A Markov model of points, games and sets gives the exact chance that each player wins from any score
\citep{klaassen2001}, so each point moves fair value by a known amount at a known moment (the \emph{swing}: mean
<< V('markov.lev') >>, << V('calc.mk.bp.lev') >> on a big point). The news reaches traders in tiers: court, video, then
score feeds << V('lat.espn') >>--<< V('lat.wta') >>\,s after the price moved. A maker whose quote
still rests after the point is picked off by whoever knows first \citep{glosten1985,budish2015,aquilina2022}, as
courtsiders do in betting markets \citep{croxson2014,brown2012}: on << V('liq.stale.n') >> live points a median
<< V('liq.stale_pre') >> of quotes still rested at stale prices 0.25\,s before the reprice
(<< V('liq.stale_post') >> after 0.5\,s), and Polymarket holds each taker order << V('venue.delay') >>\,s.

\begin{hypbox}
\textbf{H6 (pre-registered).} We expect wallets that trade within 3\,s of a score move in Polymarket ATP and WTA match
markets to earn a positive net \emph{markout} over 30\,s (profit at the price 30\,s later, after fees), because the
other side is a maker who has not yet cancelled a stale quote; the edge persists because physics and data licences
decide who learns first. Wallets picked on past months then stay positive every month and out of sample; H6 fails if
the markout is \(\leq 0\) in over a third of months, or out of sample. H1--H4 were committed before any result
(\texttt{<< V('prereg.hyp.commit') >>}); H6, written after the in-sample tier results (D5), was frozen before any
held-out read.
\end{hypbox}

\textbf{Will the edge last?}\enspace The fast tier's net edge fell << V('pers.slope.net') >>¢ a month (CI
<< V('pers.slope.net.ci') >>), mostly as the fee rose; before fees there is no clear trend (<< V('pers.slope.gross') >>¢,
CI << V('pers.slope.gross.ci') >>). A line hits zero << V('pers.zero.months') >> months after September (CI
<< V('pers.zero.ci') >>), but a May step (picked by the data) fits as well with no trend: we cannot tell if the edge is
dying, though a fee above << V('pers.befee') >> (today << V('fee.rate') >>) would end it, and entrants split the pool
(one wallet's monthly share fell from << V('pers.pool.pw.janmay') >> to << V('pers.pool.pw.aug') >>).

