% ======================================================================================== 4 Methodology
\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}
\para{The fast tier and v2} Each month a wallet joins the fast tier if, in earlier months, it traded at least
<< V('pol.q_prints') >> times 0--3\,s after a score move (a \(\geq\)<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢ move of the 10\,s price
from its prior 60\,s) in << V('pol.q_matches') >>+ matches (\(t > << V('pol.q_t') >>\)).
v2 copies their trades, sized by resolution risk (best of << V('sel.sizing.n') >> rules, walk-forward), with
<< V('risk.order_usd') >> orders and << V('risk.netcap') >> shares net per match, held to resolution. Copying is not
new; we add picking wallets by speed relative to the point, the sizing and limits, and the test that a copier loses:
v2 prices speed and is not our trade.

\para{Being fast ourselves} A CV model watches the video, calls the point early and sends a taker order before the stale
quote is cancelled. Without video of the matches we trade, we simulate it on past points with an assumed feed latency
(licensed feed not purchased); all other parameters are measured. The order wins only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
  V + \delta + D - \ell \;<\; L + R \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad V \;<\; L + R - D - \delta + \ell ,
\end{equation}
with \(V\) the feed delay, \(\delta\) our pipeline plus network, \(D\) the << V('venue.delay') >>\,s hold, \(\ell\) how
early the CV calls, \(L\) the \emph{stamp lag} (landing to official stamp) and \(R\) the reprice relative to the stamp
(median << V('lat.book_vs_stamp_signed') >>\,s). Nobody publishes \(L\): we \emph{pre-registered}
<< V('cv.pre.lag') >>\,s and also show << V('cv.cal.lag') >>\,s, a \emph{post hoc} estimate from one day. The trades
are past points where the price later moved \(\geq\)<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢: \emph{selected on outcomes, not ex ante}.

\para{Vision and speed} On held-out TrackNet broadcast games one TV camera's out calls were right
<< V('bt.call.0') >>, << V('bt.call.33') >> and << V('bt.call.67') >> at 0, 33 and 67\,ms ahead, but only bounce-time
calls met our 95\% rule. Our live engine makes about << V('cv.phantom.hr') >> phantom calls an hour outside play
(<< V('gate.hr') >> with a rally gate): not yet safe to trade. With a simulated 1\,s feed, our << V('e2e.ours') >>\,ms
pipeline, << V('e2e.net') >>\,ms of network and the hold, an order can execute << V('e2e.total') >>\,ms after the point
(p99 << V('e2e.p99') >>\,ms; built, not sent), inside the 3\,s bar. A teammate's London server saw the book first on
<< V('vultr.lon_first') >> of << V('vultr.matched') >> messages (clock-corrected); with measured cold REST round trips
(<< V('vultr.lon.rtt') >>\,ms London, << V('vultr.fl.rtt') >>\,ms Florida) the order executes in
<< V('e2e.lon.total') >>--<< V('e2e.fl.total') >>\,ms (<< V('e2e.worst.total') >>\,ms at worst; not order latency).

\para{Costs and validation} Each match charges its own taker fee \(f(q) = r\,q(1-q)\) a share, \(10{,}000\,r(1-q)\) bps
of notional \citep[fee schedule]{pmapi}; today \(r = << V('fee.rate') >>\), << V('calc.fee.q50.bps') >>\,bps at 50¢.
Fees paid were << V('v2.is.fee_bps') >> and << V('v2.oos.fee_bps') >>\,bps of notional for v2 (IS, OOS) and
<< V('cv.fee_bps.range') >>\,bps for the CV trader; v2's real fills include the spread crossed, and the CV fill walks
the measured stale book. The stress doubles the fee (v2: also the spread). We tune only in sample, walk-forward.

