\section{Risk management}\label{sec:risk}
\para{Limits and the most we can lose} Orders are at most << V('risk.order_usd') >> and a match at most
<< V('risk.netcap') >> shares net at 5--95¢, which caps the open position at << V('risk.maxloss') >>, not the loss: v2
buys both tokens in << V('risk.pm.twosided.is') >> of matches IS (<< V('risk.pm.twosided.oos') >> OOS), and the worst
match made << V('risk.pm.worst.is') >> IS, << V('risk.pm.worst.oos') >> OOS. Only the backtest's \$3,000 gross cap per
match bounds a match, at << V('risk.pm.bound') >>; the live engine lacks it. A << V('risk.daily_stop') >> daily loss ends
the day. Set in advance, we stop on a stale feed or vision, or slow latency, halve size if the trailing 30-day edge falls
below << V('pol.trail_half') >>¢, and stop at zero, at a << V('pol.dd_stop') >> drawdown or a fee or hold change. None
fired in sample.

\para{Model and concentration} Wrong CV calls always fill, so out calls need confidence \(\geq\)0.95 and one false call
halts the day. The top five copied wallets carry << V('conc.top5.is') >> of v2's P\&L IS and << V('conc.top5.oos') >>
OOS. A per-wallet limit (<< V('wcap.W') >> a day, retiring wallets whose trailing edge is \(\leq 0\); backtest only),
pre-registered on IS, kept << V('wcap.is.kept') >> of IS P\&L and doubled its drawdown; once on the burned OOS it made
about the same (<< V('wcap.oos.pnl') >> vs << V('wcap.base.oos.pnl') >>; Sharpe << V('wcap.oos.sr') >> vs
<< V('wcap.base.oos.sr') >>, CIs overlap), with a better worst day but worse drawdown, and the top five still at
<< V('wcap.oos.top5') >> (the rest lose at the same rate): a loss limit, not a fix. Settlement,
US access and ticket terms add risk \citep{pmuma,pmgeoblock,itf2026}; factor risk is nil (largest
\(|t| = << V('fac.max_t') >>\)).

